/*! \page installation Installation

\attention Although the Hexed source code is published under the very permissive
[MIT License](https://github.com/ARTLab-GT/hexed/blob/main/LICENSE.txt),
compiled binaries may be linked with OpenCASCADE, causing them to be bound by the
[LGPL License](https://dev.opencascade.org/resources/licensing).
If you redistribute compiled binaries, you must observe the terms of this license.

\section install_prereqs Prerequisites

\subsection install_prereq_os OS
Currently, Hexed supports only GNU/Linux operating systems.
The instructions on this page are targeted at and have been tested on Ubuntu 24.04 (Noble Numbat),
but should be easily adapted to other Linux distributions.
Support for Windows and Mac OS are planned for future releases.

\subsection install_prereq_python Python/pip
To install Hexed, you will need Python3, and you will need to be able to install packages with
[pip](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/key_projects/#pip).
You likely already have those things, but if you don't, you can obtain them with
```{.sh}
sudo apt install python3-full
```
If you don't have root permissions, you can also [build Python from source](https://docs.python.org/3/using/unix.html).
Also, if this is your first time installing Python packages, you should create a virtual environment
(this has always been recommended, but in Ubuntu 24.04 it is now mandatory).
You can create and activate a virtual environment with the following commands:
```{.sh}
python3 -m venv my_venv
source my_venv/bin/activate
```
You will have to repeat the `sourc my_venv/bin/activate` command every time you open a new terminal,
or else you won't be able to use your `hexed` installation.
See [this tutorial](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/venv.html)
for more information about Python packages and virtual environments.

Currently, Hexed is installed via a Python package,
which includes both a Python library of helper functions and the binaries for the Hexed solver.
This could be construed as an abuse of the Python packaging system,
but to make the installation as straightforward as possible,
I wanted a packaging system that:
- nearly everyone already has
- can install executables and libraries in the user tree,
  since not everyone has root permissions on their own machine

Currently, Python packages are the only system that appears to meet these requirements.

\section install_py_pack Install from Python package (the easy way)

The easiest way to install Hexed is from via the [Python package](https://test.pypi.org/project/hexedpy/)
with [pip](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/key_projects/#pip):
```{.sh}
python3 -m pip install --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ hexedpy
```
\note You can't just do `pip install hexedpy` because, for now,
Hexed is only on the [TestPyPI](https://test.pypi.org/), not the [real PyPI](https://pypi.org/).

Once you do this, you can run Hexed with the command \ref hexecute.
You will also have access to the \ref hexedpy Python module.
Unfortunately, due to licensing considerations, this package does not include OpenCASCADE,
so you will not be able to import geometry in \ref add_geom "CAD file formats".
For that, you need to build Hexed from source, as explained below.

\section install_source Building from source (the harder way)

For optimal performance, if you want to modify the source code, or if you need OpenCASCADE support,
you will need to build Hexed from source.
Even when you do so, Hexed is still installed as a Python wheel,
so you will still need pip as explained in \ref install_prereqs.
You will also need some basic development tools including
[git](https://git-scm.com/), [GCC](https://gcc.gnu.org/), and [Make](https://www.gnu.org/software/make/).
Again, if you're reading this, you probably already have these, but if you don't, you can install them (on Ubuntu) with
```{.sh}
sudo apt install build-essential git
```

Once you have these basic prerequisites, all you have to do is:
```{.sh}
git clone https://github.com/ARTLab-GT/hexed.git
cd hexed
git checkout latest_release
python3 build.py
```
This will create a directory named `build` in which it will compile the code and create the `hexedpy` Python wheel,
and then it will install the wheel in your current environment.

Notes:
- This may take a while, especially the first time (repeat builds should be much faster).
  If you want to speed it up, see the \ref n_build_procs option.
- It will automatically download and build any dependencies you don't already have.
  See \ref auto_dependencies below for more information.
- If you make changes to the source code,
  it you _should_ be able to just rerun `build.py` and it should do a pretty good job of
  rebuilding/reinstalling the files that were affected by the change.
  However, occasionally, a repeat build might break.
  If you encounter inexplicable problems on repeat builds,
  try deleting the `build` directory and then rebuilding.

\subsection build_options Build options
The build script (`build.py`) accepts some command line arguments.
All arguments except `--build_dir` are cached in files in the build directory.
If you rerun the build, it will remember the values of any previous options you passed
until you explicitly override them (by passing the option again with a different value) or delete the build directory.
Every option takes the form `--option_name=value`.
The whole option (including `value`) must be a valid shell token,
so if `value` contains spaces you must escape them or quote it (e.g., `--some_option="some value"`).
Any unrecognized option names are simply ignored, so watch carefully for typos.

The options are listed below.
Any options which expect a path can accept an absolute path or a path relative to your current working directory (`.`).
Any options which expect a Boolean value can accept any of the following values, all of which are case insensitive
(although otherwise options are case sensitive):
- `0`, `false`, `no`, `off`, `n`, `f` all translate to False.
- `1`, `true`, `yes`, `on`, `y`, `t` all translate to True.

\subsubsection source_dir
__Expects:__ path \n
__Default:__ `.` \n
Root directory of the source code.

\subsubsection build_dir
__Expects:__ path \n
__Default:__ `./build` \n
Directory in which compiled binaries will be placed.
This is also where cached options are stored.
So, if you run the build script multiple times with different `build_dir`s,
then each time you run it in a particular `build_dir` it will remember the most recent options you used
in that `build_dir` without affecting the options used for other `build_dir`s.
This is why the option `--build_dir` isn't cached---in order to access the cached options,
the script needs to already know the `build_dir`.

\subsubsection n_build_procs
__Expects:__ Positive integer \n
__Default:__ 1 \n
When compiling, use up to this many parallel processes.
This can significantly speed up the build, but it also uses more memory.
You can easily max out your RAM this way, so use caution.
As a rule of thumb, I usually use 1 process for every 2 GB of RAM available.

\subsubsection verbose
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ False \n
Print additional diagnostic information.

\subsubsection use_system_paths
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
If True, before installing any dependencies,
the build script will first search for them in paths typically used by the system to install files.
These include
- `/usr/lib/` for libraries
- `/usr/include/` and `/usr/local/include/` for headers

\subsubsection use_env_paths
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
If True, before installing any dependencies,
the build script will first search for them in paths specified by environment variables.
These include:
- `PATH` for executables
- `LIBRARY_PATH`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, and `DT_RPATH` for libraries
- `INCLUDE_PATH`, and `CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH` for headers
- `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` and `PATH` (with the `bin` suffices removed) for CMake configuration files.

\subsubsection internet
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
If False, the build script will avoid internet usage to install dependencies.
As a result, if some dependencies are not available, the build process will fail instead of downloading them.
This option is generally only appropriate for repeat builds,
where time may be wasted in unnecessary internet access.

\subsubsection build_mode
__Expects:__ `release` or `debug` \n
__Default:__ `release` \n
If `release`, compile flags will be chosen to provide the best possible performance.
If `debug`, compile flags will be chosen to simplify debugging (debug symbols and sanitizers are turned on).
This option also influences the default values of some other options below.

\subsubsection max_row_size
__Expects:__ Integer \f$ \ge \f$ 2 \n
__Default:__ 8 \n
Maximum \ref basis_row_size "row size" for simulations.
The larger this number, the longer the code will take to compile
(although usually most of the time is taken by compiling \ref use_occt "OCCT").
However, attempts to use a row size larger than Hexed was compiled for will result in a runtime exception.

\subsubsection threaded
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
If True, simulations will be parallelized with OpenMP.

\subsubsection n_threads
__Expects:__ Positive integer \n
__Default:__ [maximum supported](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.html#os.cpu_count)
             by the system you're building on \n
Maximum number of threads used to parallelize the code.

\subsubsection profile
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ False \n
If True, compile for profiling with `gprof`.
This will slow down the execution.

\subsubsection use_xdmf
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
If true, compile with the XDMF library to enable writing visualization files in XDMF.

\subsubsection use_tecio
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ False \n
If true, compile with Tecplot's TecIO library to enable writing visualization files in `.szplt` format.
This requires TecIO to already be installed on your system---it won't install it automatically.

\subsubsection use_occt
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
If true, compile with the OpenCASCADE library to enable reading geometry from \ref add_geom "CAD files".
The LGPL license will apply to the compiled binaries iff this is option is true.

\subsubsection build_tests
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
Compile unit tests.

\subsubsection run_tests
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True if \ref build_mode is `debug`, otherwise false.
If true, run the unit tests immediately after completing the build.

\subsubsection test_args
__Expects:__ Any string \n
__Default:__ Empty \n
If non-empty (and \ref run_tests is true), will be passed as command-line arguments to the unit test executable.
Unit testing is done with Catch2, so may use this to interact with Catch2's
[command line interface](https://github.com/catchorg/Catch2/blob/devel/docs/command-line.md).

\subsubsection gdb
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ False \n
If true (and \ref run_tests is true), rather than running the unit tests directly
it will start a [GDB](https://sourceware.org/gdb/current/onlinedocs/gdb.html/) session
and load the unit test executable.

\subsubsection build_docs
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ False \n
If true, build the documentation, when will be placed in the directory `build_dir/doc/html`.
This is primarily for developers who wish to view their changes to the documentation.
Users may simply refer to the [online documentation](https://artlab-gt.github.io/hexed/).

\subsubsection obsessive_timing
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ False \n
Causes Hexed to perform additional timing measurements and print them to the screen on execution.
For developers.

\subsubsection build_wheel
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True if \ref build_mode is `release`, otherwise false. \n
If true, build the wheel for the `hexedpy` Python package.
Otherwise, the Hexed binaries are compiled but not assembled into any type of package.
The wheel includes:
- The \ref hexedpy Python library
- The \ref hexecute executable
- All library dependencies of `hexecute` including the \ref hexed library

\subsubsection install_wheel
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True \n
If \ref build_wheel is true, then install the Python wheel in your current Python environment after building it.
If \ref build_wheel is false, has no effect.

\subsubsection architecture
__Expects:__ Architecture name, `native`, or `any` \n
__Default:__ `native` \n
CPU architecture to compile for.
Compiling for a specific architecture may improve performance on that architecture
but makes the executable incompatible with other architectures.
`native` implies that it will compile for the architecture of the machine you're building on.
`any` implies that the executable shall be compatible with any architecture, at the potential cost of performance.
Thus far, I have not conducted sufficient investigation to determine to what extent this actually affects performance.

\subsubsection sanitize
__Expects:__ Boolean \n
__Default:__ True if \ref build_mode is `debug`, otherwise false
If true, compile with
[sanitizer flags](https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Instrumentation-Options.html#index-fsanitize_003daddress).

\subsection auto_dependencies Automatically-installed dependencies
Below is a list of source code dependencies of Hexed.
The build script will automatically install any which you don't already have in the build tree (not system-wide).
If you wish to supply your own version of any of them, simply make sure the header, library, and CMake files
can be found in the standard \ref use_system_paths "system paths" or \ref use_env_paths "environment variables".
The build script isn't smart enough to check the version (yet),
so the versions listed are just the versions that will be installed if the dependency isn't found.
- [Eigen 3.4.0](https://eigen.tuxfamily.org/index.php?title=Main_Page)
- [HDF5 1.14.4.3](https://www.hdfgroup.org/solutions/hdf5/)
- [XDMF](https://www.xdmf.org/index.php/Main_Page)
- [OpenCASCADE 7.8.0](https://dev.opencascade.org/doc/overview/html/)
- [Catch2 3.6.0](https://github.com/catchorg/Catch2?tab=readme-ov-file)

Dependencies of the dependencies are not listed but are also installed automatically.
The build script will also automatically obtain dependencies of the build process itself,
including [Doxygen](https://www.doxygen.nl/download.html) and some Python packages.

*/
